  "live_records": {
    "q-163794": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163794",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/f82404522255a2e5b8be8b45d39062bd2239c15294024c02d6d4ad3dd3f44809.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.5.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum point is $(-1,2,-2.5).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/29d2b703b9c17aa69bb41076e4bf40f509af4b44bd3ade14dbdeaec7cad25120.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
