    "q-162706": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162706",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/e1f3255f450e4761e20b64c0782a241a7d73637c9e276fa9c1e07227f26cf4e2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ and $(x,y)=(1,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/1d257445551687ded71409efdce1b8695a9813a73ecb0fde5b6f4efccc6c15be.png)",
