    "q-164154": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164154",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/81604581fae8b25776727eca4450caa7d48c42a781706455ed585d5cfc4a25fa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?",
      "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence $A$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/3651b43178819666002775a26071a0bd2c9cb48b58a3c5516c38e085d50fc4a6.png)",
